      "text": "Since each die has 6 even numbers (2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12) and 6 odd numbers (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11) on each side, the probability of rolling an even number on one single dice is $\\dfrac{6}{12}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, and the probability of rolling an odd number on one single dice is also $\\dfrac{6}{12}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\nGiven 5 dice, let's find the probability of having exactly 2 dice show an even number. First, we calculate the number of ways to choose 2 dice from the 5 to show even numbers. This can be done using the binomial coefficient:\n\n\\[ C(5, 2) = \\dfrac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\dfrac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10 \\]\n\nNext, for each of these 2 chosen dice, the probability of showing an even number is $\\dfrac{1}{2}$, and for the remaining 3 dice, the probability of showing an odd number is also $\\dfrac{1}{2}$. Therefore, the probability of having exactly 2 dice show even numbers and the other 3 dice show odd numbers is:\n\n\\[ P(\\text{2 even and 3 odd}) = C(5, 2) \\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2 \\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = 10 \\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^5 = 10 \\times \\dfrac{1}{32} = \\dfrac{10}{32} = \\dfrac{5}{16} \\]\n\nSo, the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is $\\boxed{\\dfrac{5}{16}}$.",
