      "text": "To find the probability that exactly two out of five 12-sided dice show an even number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the probability of an even number on one die:**\n   Each die has 12 faces, numbered from 1 to 12. The even numbers are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12. There are 6 even numbers out of 12 possible outcomes. Therefore, the probability that a single die shows an even number is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{even number}) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   Similarly, the probability that a single die shows an odd number is also:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{odd number}) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose which two dice show even numbers:**\n   We need to choose 2 dice out of the 5 to show even numbers. The number of ways to choose 2 dice out of 5 is given by the binomial coefficient:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2!3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the probability that exactly two dice show even numbers and the other three dice show odd numbers:**\n   The probability that a specific pair of dice show even numbers is:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n   The probability that the other three dice show odd numbers is:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = \\frac{1}{8}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the probability that exactly two dice show even numbers is:\n   \\[\n   10 \\times \\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{8} = 10 \\times \\frac{1}{32} = \\frac{10}{32} = \\frac{5}{16}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Final answer:**\n   The probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}\n   \\]",
