      "text": "To find the probability that exactly two of the five 12-sided dice show an even number, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the probability of rolling an even number on a single die.**\n   Each die has 12 faces, numbered from 1 to 12. Half of these numbers are even, and the other half are odd. Therefore, the probability of rolling an even number on a single die is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{even}) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   Similarly, the probability of rolling an odd number on a single die is also:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{odd}) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose 2 dice out of 5 to show an even number.**\n   This is a combination problem where we choose 2 dice out of 5. The number of ways to do this is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the probability of a specific set of 2 dice showing even numbers and the other 3 dice showing odd numbers.**\n   The probability that a specific set of 2 dice show even numbers and the other 3 dice show odd numbers is:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^3 = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^5 = \\frac{1}{32}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total probability.**\n   The total probability is the number of ways to choose 2 dice out of 5, multiplied by the probability of a specific set of 2 dice showing even numbers and the other 3 dice showing odd numbers:\n   \\[\n   10 \\times \\frac{1}{32} = \\frac{10}{32} = \\frac{5}{16}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}\n\\]",
