      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that exactly two out of five 12-sided dice show an even number, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the probability of rolling an even number on a single die:**\n   A 12-sided die has the numbers 1 through 12. The even numbers in this range are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12. There are 6 even numbers out of 12 total numbers.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{even number}) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the probability of rolling an odd number on a single die is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{odd number}) = 1 - P(\\text{even number}) = 1 - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose which 2 dice out of 5 show an even number:**\n   We use the binomial coefficient to find the number of ways to choose 2 dice out of 5 to show even numbers.\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2!3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability of a specific sequence of 2 even and 3 odd numbers:**\n   The probability of any specific sequence of 2 even numbers and 3 odd numbers is:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^5 = \\frac{1}{32}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine the number of ways to choose the dice and the probability of a specific sequence:**\n   The total probability is the product of the number of ways to choose the dice and the probability of any specific sequence.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total probability} = \\binom{5}{2} \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^5 = 10 \\times \\frac{1}{32} = \\frac{10}{32} = \\frac{5}{16}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}\n\\]",
